 succeeded in 0ms:
     1	# Moving-Throat PDE — Stage 112: Exact Robin–Mixed Compensation Law
     2	
     3	## Goal
     4	
     5	Combine the geometric Robin core and the hidden mixed side-channel and determine whether an explicit compensated moving-throat outlet can preserve the canonical outgoing quadrupole branch.
     6	
     7	## Hybrid outlet model
     8	
     9	Take
    10	\[
    11	\boxed{
    12	\Lambda_2^{\rm hyb}(z)
    13	=
    14	\Lambda_2^{\rm out}(z)
    15	+
    16	ho_R
    17	-
    18	\frac{\sigma_W}{1-\kappa_W z^2-i\gamma_W z^5}
    19	+O(z^6).
    20	}
    21	\]
    22	Expanding,
    23	\[
    24	L_0=-3+\rho_R-\sigma_W,
    25	\qquad
    26	L_2=\frac13-\sigma_W\kappa_W,
    27	\qquad
    28	L_4=\frac19-\sigma_W\kappa_W^2,
    29	\qquad
    30	L_5=\frac19-\sigma_W\gamma_W.
    31	\]
    32	
    33	## Exact canonical-even solutions
    34	
    35	Imposing the canonical conservative even fingerprint,
    36	\[
    37	-\frac{L_2}{L_0}=\frac19,
    38	\qquad
    39	\frac{L_2^2}{L_0^2}-\frac{L_4}{L_0}=\frac{4}{81},
    40	\]
    41	yields exactly two branches:
    42	\[
    43	\boxed{\rho_R=\sigma_W,\qquad \kappa_W=0,}
    44	\]
    45	or
    46	\[
    47	\boxed{\rho_R=4\sigma_W,\qquad \kappa_W=\frac13.}
    48	\]
    49	
    50	The first is a trivial cancellation branch: the static Robin shift simply cancels the static mixed loading.
    51	The second is the nontrivial compensated branch.
    52	
    53	## Nontrivial compensated branch
    54	
    55	On
    56	\[
    57	\rho_R=4\sigma_W,
    58	\qquad
    59	\kappa_W=\frac13,
    60	\]
    61	one finds
    62	\[
    63	\Lambda_2^{\rm hyb}(z)
    64	=
    65	-3(1-\sigma_W)
    66	+
    67	\frac{1-\sigma_W}{3}z^2
    68	+
    69	\frac{1-\sigma_W}{9}z^4
    70	+
    71	 i\left(\frac19-\sigma_W\gamma_W\right)z^5
    72	+O(z^6).
    73	\]
    74	So the exact outgoing-normalization factor is
    75	\[
    76	\boxed{
    77	\chi_Q^{\rm hyb}
    78	=
    79	\frac{1-9\sigma_W\gamma_W}{1-\sigma_W}.
    80	}
    81	\]
    82	Canonical outgoing normalization is preserved iff
    83	\[
    84	\boxed{\gamma_W=\frac19.}
    85	\]
    86	With that value,
    87	\[
    88	\boxed{
    89	\Lambda_2^{\rm hyb}(z)=(1-\sigma_W)\Lambda_2^{\rm out}(z)+O(z^6),
    90	}
    91	\]
    92	so the whole hybrid outlet collapses to the harmless pure-scale deformation class.
    93	
    94	## Branch-selection data
    95	
    96	On the nontrivial compensated branch, the net Stage-92 deformation data are
    97	\[
    98	\boxed{(b,a_0,a_5)=\left(0,\,3\sigma_W,\,-\sigma_W\gamma_W\right).}
    99	\]
   100	Hence the linearized preservation condition becomes
   101	\[
   102	\frac{a_0}{3}+9a_5=\sigma_W(1-9\gamma_W)=0,
   103	\]
   104	again giving
   105	\[
   106	\boxed{\gamma_W=\frac19.}
   107	\]
   108	
   109	## Consequence
   110	
   111	This is the first explicit compensated moving-throat outlet model that preserves the canonical outgoing quadrupole branch. It shows that neither a pure geometric Robin core nor a naive hidden mixed pole is sufficient by itself, but a specific Robin–mixed balance law can reduce the whole deformation to a pure mouth renormalization, which is exactly the robust class already identified in Stages 90–92.

